// Wraps cuGraphInstantiate, bound as cuGraphInstantiateWithFlags.
func (g Graph) Instantiate(flags uint64) (GraphExec, error) {
	return graphInstantiate(g, flags)
}

// InstantiateWithParams Creates an executable graph from a graph.
//
// Instantiates hGraph as an executable graph according to the instantiateParams structure. The graph is validated for any structural constraints or intra-node constraints which were not previously validated. If instantiation is successful, a handle to the instantiated graph is returned in phGraphExec. instantiateParams controls the behavior of instantiation and subsequent graph launches, as well as returning more detailed information in the event of an error. [GraphInstantiateParams] is defined as: The flags field controls the behavior of instantiation and subsequent graph launches. Valid flags are: If hGraph contains any allocation or free nodes, there can be at most one executable graph in existence for that graph at a time. An attempt to instantiate a second executable graph before destroying the first with [GraphExec.Destroy] will result in an error. The same also applies if hGraph contains any device-updatable kernel nodes. If hGraph contains kernels which call device-side cudaGraphLaunch() from multiple contexts, this will result in an error. Graphs instantiated for launch on the device have additional restrictions which do not apply to host graphs: In the event of an error, the result_out and hErrNode_out fields will contain more information about the nature of the error. Possible error reporting includes: If instantiation is successful, result_out will be set to [GraphInstantiateSuccess], and hErrNode_out will be set to NULL. See also: [NewGraph], [Graph.Instantiate], [GraphExec.Destroy]
//
//   - instantiateParams: Instantiation parameters
//
// Wraps cuGraphInstantiateWithParams.
func (g Graph) InstantiateWithParams(instantiateParams *GraphInstantiateParams) (GraphExec, error) {
	return graphInstantiateWithParams(g, instantiateParams)
}

// ReleaseUserObject Release a user object reference from a graph.
//
// Releases user object references owned by a graph. See CUDA User Objects in the CUDA C++ Programming Guide for more information on user objects. See also: [NewUserObject], [UserObject.Retain], [UserObject.Release], [Graph.RetainUserObject], [NewGraph]
//
//   - object: The user object to release a reference for
//   - count: The number of references to release, typically 1. Must be nonzero and not larger than INT_MAX
//
// Wraps cuGraphReleaseUserObject.
func (g Graph) ReleaseUserObject(object UserObject, count uint32) error {
	return graphReleaseUserObject(g, object, count)
}

// RemoveDependencies Removes dependency edges from a graph.
//
// The number of dependencies to be removed is defined by numDependencies. Elements in from and to at corresponding indices define a dependency. Each node in from and to must belong to hGraph. If numDependencies is 0, elements in from and to will be ignored. Specifying an edge that does not exist in the graph, with data matching edgeData, results in an error. edgeData is nullable, which is equivalent to passing default (zeroed) data for each edge. Dependencies cannot be removed from graphs which contain allocation or free nodes. Any attempt to do so will return an error. See also: [Graph.AddDependencies], [Graph.GetEdges], [GraphNode.GetDependencies], [GraphNode.GetDependentNodes]
//
//   - from: Array of nodes that provide the dependencies
//   - to: Array of dependent nodes
//   - edgeData: Optional array of edge data. If NULL, edge data is assumed to be default (zeroed)
//   - numDependencies: Number of dependencies to be removed
//
// Wraps cuGraphRemoveDependencies, bound as cuGraphRemoveDependencies_v2.
func (g Graph) RemoveDependencies(from *GraphNode, to *GraphNode, edgeData *GraphEdgeData, numDependencies uint) error {
	return graphRemoveDependencies(g, from, to, edgeData, numDependencies)
}

// RetainUserObject Retain a reference to a user object from a graph.
//
// Creates or moves user object references that will be owned by a CUDA graph. See CUDA User Objects in the CUDA C++ Programming Guide for more information on user objects. See also: [NewUserObject], [UserObject.Retain], [UserObject.Release], [Graph.ReleaseUserObject], [NewGraph]
//
//   - object: The user object to retain a reference for
//   - count: The number of references to add to the graph, typically 1. Must be nonzero and not larger than INT_MAX
//   - flags: The optional flag [GraphUserObjectMove] transfers references from the calling thread, rather than create new references. Pass 0 to create new references
//
// Wraps cuGraphRetainUserObject.
func (g Graph) RetainUserObject(object UserObject, count uint32, flags uint32) error {
	return graphRetainUserObject(g, object, count, flags)
}

// NewGraphChildGraphNodeGetGraph Gets a handle to the embedded graph of a child graph node.
//
// Gets a handle to the embedded graph in a child graph node. This call does not clone the graph. Changes to the graph will be reflected in the node, and the node retains ownership of the graph. Allocation and free nodes cannot be added to the returned graph. Attempting to do so will return an error. See also: [Graph.AddChildGraphNode], [GraphNode.FindInClone]
//
//   - hNode: Node to get the embedded graph for
//   - phGraph: Location to store a handle to the graph
//
// Wraps cuGraphChildGraphNodeGetGraph.
func NewGraphChildGraphNodeGetGraph(hNode GraphNode) (Graph, error) {
	return graphChildGraphNodeGetGraph(hNode)
}

// NewGraph Creates a graph.
//
// Creates an empty graph, which is returned via phGraph. See also: [Graph.AddChildGraphNode], [Graph.AddEmptyNode], [Graph.AddKernelNode], [Graph.AddHostNode], [Graph.AddMemcpyNode], [Graph.AddMemsetNode], [Graph.Instantiate], [Graph.Destroy], [Graph.GetNodes], [Graph.GetRootNodes], [Graph.GetEdges], [Graph.Clone]
//
//   - phGraph: Returns newly created graph
//   - flags: Graph creation flags, must be 0
//
// Wraps cuGraphCreate.
func NewGraph(flags uint32) (Graph, error) {
	return graphCreate(flags)
}
